// `?` and prefix `?` (if) are the same token, so the parser looks ahead to tell a
// try-unwrap from the start of a conditional. The lookahead scanned past `while`
// and found the *loop's* own `⟦`, concluding the `?` opened a conditional — the
// `?` was then left to begin the next statement, which parsed as `? while …` and
// failed with "unexpected token While", pointing at the loop rather than at the
// line above it.
//
// A loop keyword can never start an if-condition, since none of them can be bound
// as a name. Reading a line and looping on it is the ordinary shape:
n ↢ ok(0)?
seen ↢ []
while n < 3 ⟦
  seen := concat(seen, [n])
  n := n + 1
⟧
^ ⟨seen: seen, n: n⟩
